From a5e155ecfd9b71faa8f1e853d65bcc08efea7b5a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SteveDeFacto <5148995+SteveDeFacto@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 10:05:45 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Coupled shares: relaunch on an owner's resize both ways;
 fixtures read a pooled manager

Per root review of 14b245926: a partition sized for another share (an owner's
resize up OR down, or 0xc13d4b6a's dial-sized spawn below the 2% this node
counts) is relaunched in place at its share when that fits (a shrink always
does), at most once per SHARE_RELAUNCH_MIN_MS. Until then it keeps serving and
stays explicitly pending on every pass (allocatedShare + reason), counted at
the larger of the two. The resize log no longer claims a relaunch it has not
made.

Fixtures (no production behaviour): the shared fake manager takes an opt-in
ramPoolMiB (main.mjs NODE_APP_RAM_GB x 1024); hosting-caps and preclaim boot a
pooled manager with the same pool on the node; isolation-host and scan-slot give
the share-accounting tests a known, empty manager view; hosting-caps DEFAULTS
compares at the published millionth. Unknown/malformed/pool-less managers are
still refused in their own tests.
